From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sun, 27 Nov 1994 19:45:08 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-437] COMPUTERS: the persistence of C

Craig Presson: 
> The market for programming close to the hardware has shrunk enormously
> in the last 10 years as the number of different hardware platforms has
> decreased.

This is simply wrong.  The number of new platforms has grown with the
RISC revolution, and we have games programming on top of that -- an
applications area where programming close to the hardware is especially
highly prized.  Unix porting (read: rewriting most of the kernel
to get something that does databases at least halfway efficiently)
is still just about as big as it ever was, and any jobs lost there
have have gone into low-level programming of database and other 
mass-market applications programs themselves.  We've seen a huge blossoming
in Mac and Windows applications programming in C/C++ over the last
ten years which dwarfs any loss in OS kernel programming.  Not
to mention all those C code demons working on Chicago, WindowsNT,
the latest IBM/Apple systems software gigaproject, etc. -- all in
C.  We've seen a major blossoming in networking applications, the
vast majority of non-custom ones done in, you guess it, C.

What has changed is that most of the people who programmed in
assembler 10 years ago now program in C, because of its portability,
optimizing compilers, and (contrary to your statement above)
the proliferation of new platforms and their accompanying machine
languages, which makes learning each new hardware language increasingly
a losing bet.

> In short, this is an area where I speak from experience...

As opposed to whom?  I've spent most of my career in systems 
software engineering.

