Subject: [#94-11-441] PREDICTION: a crucial tool in the Extropian kit

Nancy Lebowitz: 
> What's information distance?

Here are the several standard kinds:

* The bit difference between two strings, ie the number bits needed
to go from one to the other
* The shortest algorithm to change one bit string to the other.
* Algorithmic distance between several originator data structures
and a resultant data structure.

The third is the most general, and what I'm refering to in my argument.
Designs, tooling interfaces, etc. can all be represented as bit
strings, though it is not necessary to literally do so to 
visualize information distance between two designs or between
tooling infrastructures.

For more on information distance see Li & Vitanyi, _An Introduction 
to Kolmogorov Complexity and Its Applications_, 1993.
	
> What's the line of argument that it's unlikely for there
> to be a major new religion?

This one's easy: pick a random interval of 15 years out of the
historial record, and the probability that a new religion has
spread widely enough during that interval to usurp the place
of a major religion is practically nil.  Modern travel and 
communications may have changed the frequency, but not
by much, as the persistence and even growth of Christianity
in the Americas, Islam in Asia, etc. during the 20th century
indicates.  There has been a trend towards paganism and
atheism since soon after the Renaissance, to the point where
much of Europe is now at least agnostic.  But this is a several
hundred year old trend that has often been temporarily reversed,
and indeed is probably in such a setback right now.  It is
not something that is going to sweep away the major religions
over the next few decades.

> What's the line of argument that smart drugs couldn't make
> a big difference in 15 years

I didn't argue this at all; in fact I think it's one of the more
probable near term scenarios, since there are already strongly
memory enhancing drugs, neural growth factors, etc. in the
laboratories.  It might end up being a widely suppressed black 
market phenomenon, though, as the current medical establishment (AMA, 
drug companies, etc.) have reached at least a public consesus that 
such drugs are ethically unacceptable.  Instead they are being 
developed to target the Alzheimer's et. al. market.  In our political 
system such ethical biases often get translated into viscious demonizing 
rhetoric, and then into law.  The importance of getting the medical ethics
arguments on our side, that apply in the current dominant memetic
environment, cannot be emphasized enough; an improvement in
medical ethics is perhaps the most important contribution the 
Extropians movement can make.

