On Sunday CSPAN showed a talk presented by Ms. Paglia. It was delivered to a
University audience (Georgetown I think) and she used the same joke about
Dante that Sheaffer mentioned in his review. Since CSPAN shows programs
repeatedly it might appear again. I was underwhelmed by Paglia's performance
and by the several opinion pieces I have read by her.  She does attack
speech codes, humanities professors at `elite' schools, womens studies
programs, pornography restrictions, etc.  These kinds of `culture war'
topics are exciting, interesting and important to many people.  

For me these topics are sometimes engaging but when I am in a contemplative
mood I consider them too limited, transient, and parochial to be very
compelling.  If I want to think about gender issues then I wonder about the
implications of being able to easily switch genders or create new genders or
non-genders - literally not metaphorically.  If I want to think about
pornography then I think about what it would mean to be able to precisely
control your own pleasure sensing apparatus through direct neural control.
You can see the pattern. Why get exercised about questions that are
old and boring when new and wild questions beckon?    gfs

